\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Why the periodic trace needs gauge projection}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take a graph with one edge and two distinct vertices, with
#{G=\mathbb Z_2}, #{V=0} and the
[sign kernel](fdip-0081) #{k(u)=\ue^{\beta u}}, #{\beta>0}.
The link space has orthonormal basis #{1,u}, with
##{T1=(\cosh\beta)1,\qquad Tu=(\sinh\beta)u.}
The two endpoint gauge transformations act by
#{u\mapsto h_tuh_s}. Their action is transitive on the two
link configurations. Thus the [gauge projector](fdip-0083)
satisfies #{P1=1} and #{Pu=0}, and the physical space consists
exactly of constants.}

\p{For every #{N\ge1}, the projected and unprojected traces are
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Tr}(PT^N)&=(\cosh\beta)^N,\\
\operatorname{Tr}(T^N)&=(\cosh\beta)^N+(\sinh\beta)^N.
\end{aligned}}
They are different because #{\beta>0}. In the temporal-link
integral, each independent endpoint average gives
##{\frac14\sum_{h_s,h_t=\pm1}
 \ue^{\beta U h_sU'h_t}=\cosh\beta.}
The remaining normalized link integrals contribute one, reproducing
the projected answer. Dropping #{P} would retain the nonphysical
sign mode and change this finite periodic law.}
